WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.126 --> 00:00:03.155
[Music starts] [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:03.155 --> 00:00:08.127
[Lisa Federer, PhD] Open Science is really
an umbrella term that includes a lot of different practices

00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:16.143
that enable people to access the results of
science.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:23.380
[Maryam Zaringhalam, PhD] When I first learned
about Open Science, I think it was best described

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.674
to me as really resting on three pillars.

00:00:26.674 --> 00:00:32.316
You have Open Data, meaning that the data
are made openly available, that they're shared.

00:00:32.316 --> 00:00:38.430
You have Open Source, which means that the
underlying analytical tools and software and

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.028
code are made openly available.

00:00:41.028 --> 00:00:46.388
And then you have Open Access, which is that
publications, that are sort of the synthesis

00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:52.359
of the data and the results that came after
the analysis, are also made openly available

00:00:52.359 --> 00:00:53.542
for folks to read.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:01.613
[Federer] NLM is really interested in creating
expertise and knowledge about data.

00:01:01.613 --> 00:01:07.118
Not just in, you know, the research institution
or in libraries, but also preparing the public

00:01:07.118 --> 00:01:08.243
to be more data savvy.

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:15.200
[Zaringhalam] Having as many people as possible
- who are able to access the data, who are

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.970
able to look under the hood - makes science
more rigorous.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:24.533
Because, you have more folks who are able
to sort of tap into this data, tap into your code, 

00:01:24.533 --> 00:01:29.729
have access to the publications that
result from it, and engage in this consensus

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:35.534
building that is so incredibly important and
foundational for science to move forward.

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:39.810
[Federer] We're in a position that we can
talk with publishers about why open access

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.119
is important, or talk with them about why
they should be instituting data citation,

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:47.563
which would make it easier for us to track
data.

00:01:53.504 --> 00:02:00.836
We have a really unique position, as a medical
library of our sort of standing, to really

00:02:00.836 --> 00:02:05.567
help push forward some of the practices that
will lead to more open science.

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:16.547
[Music]

00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:17.278
[Music fades to silence]

